t completes a lap every 12 minutes, and the second cyclist completes a lap every 16 minutes. We start by finding the prime factorizations of 12 and 16:\n\n\\[ 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\]\n\\[ 16 = 2^4 \\]\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations. Thus, the LCM of 12 and 16 is:\n\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(12, 16) = 2^4 \\times 3 = 48 \\]\n\nThis means that the two racers will cross the starting line together every 48 minutes. Since they both started at 12:15 PM, we add 48 minutes to this time to find the next time they cross the starting line together:\n\n\\[ 12:15 \\text{ PM} + 48 \\text{ minutes} = 1:03 \\text{ PM} \\]\n\nThus, the next time they cross the starting line together is \\(\\boxed{1:03}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1:03}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:187"}
